    "analysis": "Entropy at K_s=3 (4.71) and K_m=4 (4.85) are similar, and both are close to L12 (5.04). Position correlation is uniformly weak (~0.17) across all layers. This means the ratio A_mid/(A_shallow+eps) is NOT correcting for positional bias (there is minimal positional bias at any layer), nor is it correcting entropy differences (entropies are similar). Instead, dividing by A_shallow distorts the saliency signal, removing information about which tokens are genuinely important."
  },
  "conclusion": "The shallow-layer normalization (A_mid / (A_shallow + eps)) provides NO genuine debiasing value. At every tested configuration, it degrades performance compared to using raw A_mid directly: L4: -25.46 points, L12 K_s=3: -11.69 points, L12 K_s=2: -5.22 points. Even the softer weighted combination approach hurts by -2.08 points at L12. The hypothesis that shallow-layer attention can serve as a positional bias corrector is REFUTED: Phase-0 showed position correlation is uniformly weak (~0.17) across all layers, meaning there is no position-specific bias to correct. The denominator A_shallow does non-trivial work, but that work is HARMFUL -- it divides out part of the saliency signal rather than removing positional bias."
